WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:05.540
It's one of the most hyped games that never
existed: Half-Life 2: Episode Three.

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:09.180
Billed as the conclusion to the Half-Life
saga, it was originally supposed to hit the

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:12.000
shelves before Christmas of 2007.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.720
Then developer Valve delayed it while they
expanded it into a full-length sequel, Half-Life

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:17.720
3.

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:18.720
And delayed it.

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:19.720
And delayed it.

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:21.680
And, well, fans are still waiting.

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:25.070
Here's a look at why we never got to play
Half-Life 3.

00:00:25.070 --> 00:00:26.910
The hype is too big

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:31.390
Since the original announcement of Half-Life
2: Episode Three, Valve has released several

00:00:31.390 --> 00:00:36.940
blockbuster hit games, including Portal and
Portal 2, Team Fortress 2 and Left 4 Dead.

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:41.510
But despite the success of these titles, the
game that eventually became known as Half-Life

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:46.079
3 was never rolled out to the masses, instead
earning the top slot among the most famous

00:00:46.079 --> 00:00:47.660
pieces of vaporware.

00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:52.440
And as time went on, the company line on Episode
Three from creator Gabe Newell evolved from

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:57.120
firm commitment to vague mentions to complete
refusals to comment whatsoever.

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:01.840
"The main thing that we’re, uh, working
on right now after we get the Orange Box out

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:06.280
the door is going to be episode 3, so we can
finish of this trilogy and…"

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:10.900
"Well, I can’t really say anything about
Half-Life 3."

00:01:10.909 --> 00:01:15.969
In a Reddit AMA in January 2017, Newell said
that "it's hard for me to look at them as

00:01:15.969 --> 00:01:18.520
anything other than a series of things I regret."

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:21.319
That doesn't sound promising.

00:01:21.319 --> 00:01:23.219
The main writer retired

00:01:23.219 --> 00:01:28.590
In early 2016, Marc Laidlaw—the main writer
and creative voice behind the entire Half-Life

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:32.420
series—retired from Valve after 18 years
with the company.

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:36.670
When asked what his departure from Valve meant
for the completion of Half-Life 3, Laidlaw

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:39.779
answered, "I don't and can't entirely know.

00:01:39.779 --> 00:01:41.759
Half-Life is fully owned by Valve.

00:01:41.759 --> 00:01:44.259
It came into existence before my arrival.

00:01:44.259 --> 00:01:48.819
Where Valve may choose to take it in the future
is not in my hands."

00:01:48.819 --> 00:01:50.119
Internal politics

00:01:50.119 --> 00:01:54.460
According to an anonymous Valve employee who
spoke with Game Informer, the dynamic way

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:58.859
the employees choose projects contributed
to Half-Life 3 never getting off the ground.

00:01:58.859 --> 00:02:03.149
Developers, artists, story writers, and other
team members can literally take their desk

00:02:03.149 --> 00:02:06.250
and move it to whatever project interests
them the most.

00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:09.769
This means internal politics can really make
or break a game.

00:02:09.769 --> 00:02:14.250
The anonymous source estimates that various
iterations of Half-Life 3 have been in development

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:15.549
as early as 2005.

00:02:15.549 --> 00:02:19.939
"I've heard that some teams have had two to
three people working on it, and they eventually

00:02:19.939 --> 00:02:25.540
ran into a wall, and some teams may have gotten
up to 30 or 40 people before it was scrapped.

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:29.049
Some people—I don't want to name names—were
excited about their projects.

00:02:29.049 --> 00:02:31.430
They've had some different thoughts about
what it should be.

00:02:31.430 --> 00:02:35.659
Every time a Half-Life project gets some gravity
and then collapses, it becomes harder for

00:02:35.659 --> 00:02:38.040
the next one to start up."

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:39.319
Steambath

00:02:39.319 --> 00:02:43.209
Valve isn't seriously in the business of making
video games anymore, and they haven't been

00:02:43.209 --> 00:02:44.400
for some time.

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:49.189
With the exception of The Lab—a set of free
mini-games that shipped in 2016 with the HTC

00:02:49.189 --> 00:02:55.069
Vive—Valve hasn't actually created a title
since Dota 2, back in 2013.

00:02:55.069 --> 00:02:59.400
It's worth noting that many of Valve's franchises,
including Left 4 Dead and Portal, started

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:03.480
out in the hands of other companies—companies
which Valve then acquired.

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:09.599
Similarly, Counterstrike, Dota 2, Day of Defeat,
and Team Fortress all started as mods—mods

00:03:09.599 --> 00:03:10.890
which Valve then acquired.

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:12.939
Do you notice a pattern here?

00:03:12.939 --> 00:03:17.359
Valve's only game franchise that they conceptualized
and developed from the very beginning was

00:03:17.359 --> 00:03:21.049
the Half-Life saga, which launched the company
into the big leagues.

00:03:21.049 --> 00:03:25.040
Since then, they've moved on to more profitable
ventures, such as Steam.

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:30.219
While Valve doesn't publicly release financial
data, in 2011, a Forbes article estimated

00:03:30.219 --> 00:03:36.050
that through Steam, Valve controlled between
50 and 70% of the downloadable PC game market,

00:03:36.050 --> 00:03:39.939
and that they take 30% of every sale on Steam
for themselves.

00:03:39.939 --> 00:03:45.840
That means in 2016 alone, they may have brought
in as much as $840 million distributing games

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:48.150
they didn't spend a dime to develop.

00:03:48.150 --> 00:03:52.530
Meanwhile, it's estimated that the Half Life
franchise has generated only about twice that

00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:57.400
much over the past 19 years combined, at a
much higher cost to the company.

00:03:57.400 --> 00:04:02.090
Simple math says that Half-Life 3 may literally
not be worth their time or effort.

00:04:02.090 --> 00:04:03.969
The most important character died

00:04:03.969 --> 00:04:08.810
Okay, at this point, some of you are yelling
at your screen, "Gordon is still alive, you

00:04:08.810 --> 00:04:09.810
fool!"

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:13.889
But Eli Vance—not Gordon Freeman—was actually
the most important character of the Half-Life

00:04:13.889 --> 00:04:14.889
saga.

00:04:14.889 --> 00:04:18.359
He was essentially a living MacGuffin who
drove the entire plot.

00:04:18.359 --> 00:04:19.359
Think about it.

00:04:19.359 --> 00:04:23.180
In Half-Life 2, your first objective is to
go meet Eli Vance.

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:26.230
Then, when he gets kidnapped, your objective
is to rescue him.

00:04:26.230 --> 00:04:29.389
But then he gets captured again, and so you
have to go rescue him again.

00:04:29.389 --> 00:04:32.020
"I’ve been on the line with Alex.

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:33.720
Her father’s been captured."

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:37.580
"Eli Vance is indispensable to the liberation."

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:41.580
When you finally free him, the game ends before
he tells you anything, and you spend the next

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:46.330
two episodes basically tracking him down yet
again to find out what his big stupid secret

00:04:46.330 --> 00:04:49.130
is, which he still doesn't tell you before
he dies!

00:04:49.130 --> 00:04:54.340
Without Eli as a focus for your efforts, there's
a huge hole in franchise, and if Valve has

00:04:54.340 --> 00:04:57.770
figured out a way to fill it, they certainly
aren't saying.

00:04:57.770 --> 00:04:58.860
Say never

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:02.710
At this point, it looks as though the only
thing that could convince Valve to make another

00:05:02.710 --> 00:05:07.740
Half-Life is if the fans really, really want
it—the fans of the franchise inside Valve

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:08.800
itself, that is.

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:11.130
That's what Newell said in a 2015 podcast:

00:05:11.130 --> 00:05:16.639
"The only reason we'd go back and do like
a super-classic kind of product is if a whole

00:05:16.639 --> 00:05:21.000
bunch of people just internally at Valve said,
you know, they wanted to do it.”

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:25.770
We know exactly what that means: Half-Life
3 is never going to happen.

00:05:25.770 --> 00:05:26.800
Thanks for watching!

